<ul>
    <li>The context window is limited (at most a million tokens), so it cannot open everything.</li>
    <li>That is why only the important content is put at the very top (the metadata), stated <strong>concisely</strong>.</li>
    <li>Agents like Claude Code read this <strong>metadata first</strong>. Compared with the whole, it is the tip of the iceberg, but by skimming just these tips they quickly grasp what the content relates to.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Writing good titles and descriptions (in English)</h3>
<p>Just like a skill's <code>description</code>, it matters to write a note's <strong>title and description fields</strong> well. We are Korean, but <strong>artificial intelligence reads English faster and better</strong>, so it pays to write the metadata in English.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Context optimization</span>
    A big advantage of Obsidian is that instead of packing everything into one file, you can point to the address of another file — <strong>"for tastes, see tastes.md," "for the expert persona, look here."</strong> That way only the files needed for the work at hand get opened, which <strong>optimizes context</strong>. Borrowing Karpathy's idea, the speaker built a set of commands and skills that automate writing this metadata.
</div>

<h3>Links and Cross-Vault References</h3>
<p>Obsidian's greatest strength is <strong>links between notes</strong>. For example, a "BM25" note has all sorts of notes linked to it — an MOC (Map of Content, a knowledge map), harness engineering, logs, and more. These <strong>links are the heart of it</strong>.</p>

<h4>One address reads the linked knowledge along with it</h4>
<p>Hand artificial intelligence just the address of the "BM25" note, and it reads <strong>the rest of the notes linked to it, strung together like sausages</strong>. That automatic linking, however, only works <strong>within the same vault</strong>.</p>

