<h4>Cross-vault links — markdown links</h4>
<p>The wikilinks (<code>[[...]]</code>) used inside a vault cannot point to notes outside it, so links to an external vault have to use the <strong>markdown link form</strong> (<code>[text](address)</code>). The speaker has set up the system files so that these cross-vault links get written automatically too.</p>
<ul>
    <li>During onboarding it asks, "What is your main vault?"</li>
    <li>Give it the address of the main vault and it <strong>parses that URI value</strong> and builds the links to those addresses along the way.</li>
</ul>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Example — mobile command center</span>
    When building a "mobile command center" wiki card about putting Starlink and a battery system in a vehicle, the artificial intelligence found and linked not only the related concepts inside the wiki vault (Related) but also the <strong>related notes organized in the main vault</strong> (main vault Related). Connecting <strong>your entire knowledge system</strong> this way goes beyond the innate limitation of Obsidian vaults (links only within a vault).
</div>

<h3>Kinds of Wiki Cards and Raw Sources</h3>
<p>A researcher has to state the <strong>source</strong> of their knowledge. So raw material is held as a <strong>raw source</strong>, marking where the knowledge came from. (Automated indexing and citation through a connected reference manager such as Zotero or Bookends is also planned.)</p>

<h4>Raw source → wiki card</h4>
<p>Put articles, news stories, papers, <strong>YouTube transcripts</strong>, and the like into the vault, and the LLM reads them, extracts from them, and turns them into <strong>wiki cards</strong>. If there are five things to learn from a single article, five cards can come out of it, and you can also <strong>ingest several sources at once</strong> (3 articles + 2 YouTube videos) and let the AI compile them on its own.</p>

