<h4>Kinds of cards</h4>
<div class="tbl-wrap">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Concept</strong></td><td>A basic concept card.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Entity</strong></td><td>Proper nouns, service names, people's names, and so on.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Guide</strong></td><td>A phrase that contains concepts and entities — a how-to, like "do this with that".</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Maps / Labs</strong></td><td>Concept cards that cover the whole picture.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Capture tip — leave the purpose behind</span>
    When capturing knowledge, always write <strong>the purpose after the # (hash)</strong>. This is so you can search for it later. Since you end up capturing things and never looking at them again, it is good to leave <strong>hints that future-you can reach through search</strong>. When you hand off an ingest as well, if you have it ask "why did you collect this / what are you trying to do", it takes that intent and connects and reasons with past research in the main vault.
</div>

<h3>System Files and rules</h3>
<p>The vault holds not only notes but also <strong>system files that govern how the AI behaves</strong>. These files, carefully carved out by the instructor, are worth reading through once.</p>

<h4>Stating the author and the model</h4>
<ul>
    <li>The main author of the main vault is <strong>a human (me)</strong>.</li>
    <li>In the LLM wiki, <strong>which model wrote it</strong> is stated alongside my name. For example: "Opus 4.8 · effort X-high", "Fable", "Codex Fugu · Ultra", and so on.</li>
    <li>Recording which model made a wiki lets you <strong>compare and optimize quality</strong>. Ingesting uses a lot of tokens and time, so it is best to collect <strong>only the material that really matters</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<h4>System files in the main vault</h4>
<p>These include documents for Claude to read, documents for other agents to read, CMDS (context) related material, vault rules, writing and formatting rules, research topics, personas (brain dump), and more.</p>

<h4>core-context in the LLM wiki</h4>
<p>The LLM wiki uses a context file that holds <strong>what purpose you intend to use the wiki for</strong>. When you start onboarding, you edit <code>core-context.md</code> and fill in your name, affiliation, department, hobbies, tastes, and so on. Here too, instead of writing everything in one file, you optimize context by stating addresses like <strong>"tastes go in 취향.md, the specialist persona goes here"</strong>.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">.claude/rules — rules read like a constitution</span>
    Markdown files in the <strong><code>rules</code></strong> folder inside the <code>.claude</code> folder are <strong>read first, the moment a session starts (and obeyed strictly, like a constitution)</strong>. <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, by contrast, is consulted like a guide. So it is good to put editing rules, preferences, and important mandatory rules into <code>rules</code>.
</div>
